=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The DNS daemon (`named`/`bind9`/`dnsmasq`) on `dns_server` — the process is completely absent and nothing is listening on UDP/TCP port 53.

**Mechanism:** When `host_1_1_1_1` (or any other client) sends a DNS query for `web0.local`, `web1.local`, etc., the query packet reaches `dns_server` (10.200.0.2) at the IP layer but is silently dropped by the kernel because no socket is bound to port 53. The resolver on the client times out and returns `no_addresses`, which causes all hostname-based HTTP requests to fail with connection code `000` (unable to resolve). Both hosts are affected identically because both point to the same nameserver.

**Localization:** Fault is confined to `dns_server`: `pgrep` for all known DNS daemons returns nothing; `ss -tulpn` shows no port 53 listener; `nft list ruleset` reveals only baseline Docker NAT rules — no port-53 drop rule exists. The server itself is reachable via ICMP (confirmed 0% loss from both hosts in Phase 1), OSPF fabric is fully converged (6/6 FRR routers healthy, all neighbors Full), and no L2, ACL, or TC anomaly was found anywhere in the topology.

**Root cause:** The DNS daemon on `dns_server` is not running, leaving port 53 unmanned and making all hostname resolution fail topology-wide.

**Fix:** Start the DNS daemon on `dns_server` — e.g., `systemctl start named` (or `bind9`/`dnsmasq` depending on the installed package). No routing, OSPF, or ACL changes are needed.
=== END ===